// Study guide text used to ground the Tutor mode
window.FMAA_GUIDE = "# FMAA Study Guide  -  Section A (General Accounting & Financial Management) and Section B (Financial Statement Analysis)\n\nSource: PRC (Powers Resources Corporation) FMAA e-books, Study Sessions 1-5 (A.1-A.5, B.1-B.3). Organized by topic \u2192 key definitions \u2192 rules/formulas \u2192 worked numbers \u2192 exam traps drawn from the session quizzes.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION A  -  GENERAL ACCOUNTING AND FINANCIAL MANAGEMENT\n\n### A.1 Accounting Terminology and Definitions\n\n**Accounting (ICMA definition):** identifying, classifying, measuring, recording, and communicating, in monetary terms, transactions/events of an economic entity that are of a financial character.\n\n**Three types of accounting**\n\n| Type | Users | Standards | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Financial accounting | External (creditors, shareholders, investors, lenders, government) | US GAAP / IFRS; changes over time, differs by country | Reports required at end of each period; focuses on past |\n| Managerial accounting | Internal decision-makers (plan, evaluate, control, resource accountability) | No mandatory standards; similar across countries; stable over time | Reports issued as management needs; focuses on past AND future |\n| Tax/statutory accounting | Tax authorities | Local tax law | Beyond FMAA scope |\n\n**Exam trap (QUC 12328):** what is specific to managerial accounting only \u2192 *reports prepared based on management needs*. Financial accounting must follow standards, is issued periodically, and focuses on the past.\n\n**Forms of business organization**\n\n| Form | Separate legal entity? | Liability | Life | Other |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Sole proprietorship | No (for tax/legal) | Unlimited | Owner's life | One owner keeps all profit/loss |\n| General partnership | No | All partners unlimited | Partners' lives; any change = new partnership | Easy to form, may form without intent |\n| Limited partnership | Created by statute (LP certificate filed) | GP(s) unlimited; LP(s) limited |  -  | \u22651 GP and \u22651 LP; only GPs manage; LP name cannot appear in firm name; LP interest \u2248 a security |\n| LLP | Statutory filing | All partners limited (except personal malpractice) |  -  | Usually professional services; all may manage |\n| Corporation (C-corp) | Yes | Limited | Unlimited | Shareholders elect board; board appoints management; **double taxation** (corporate + dividends); public corps regulated (SEC, PCAOB) |\n| LLC |  -  | Limited + preferential tax treatment (with conditions) |  -  | |\n| S-corporation (US) |  -  |  -  |  -  | Not taxed at corporate level; \u226475 shareholders; one class of stock |\n| Joint venture |  -  |  -  |  -  | Partnership arrangement of 2+ parties for a specific purpose (QUC 11942) |\n| Professional corporation |  -  |  -  |  -  | Limited to licensed professionals |\n| Nonprofit |  -  |  -  |  -  | Public benefit; usually tax-exempt; surplus reinvested, profit not primary goal |\n\n**The accounting equation:** Assets = Liabilities + Owner's Equity (Dr = Cr + Cr)\n\n- **Assets:** probable future economic benefits obtained/controlled from past transactions (cash, A/R, inventory, prepayments, PP&E). Normal **debit** balance.\n- **Liabilities:** probable future sacrifices of economic benefits from present obligations (A/P, N/P, taxes payable, loans). Normal **credit** balance.\n- **Equity:** residual interest in assets after liabilities = contributed capital + retained earnings. Normal **credit** balance.\n\n**Glossary (ICMA):** business transaction, recordkeeping, debit (left; \u2191asset/expense, \u2193liability/equity), credit (right; \u2191liability/equity, \u2193asset/expense), journal (book of original entry, chronological), account (record in GL), ledger (book of final entry), general ledger (primary record containing all accounts), financial statement (BS, IS, CFS), accounting period, cash flows, profit (revenues > expenses), loss (expenses > revenues).\n\n**Accounting principles**\n\n1. **Cash vs. accrual.** Cash basis: record when cash received/paid (tolerated for small businesses). Accrual basis (GAAP/IFRS): revenue when *earned*, expense when *incurred*, regardless of cash. Examples: haircut Dec 28 paid Jan 3 \u2192 accrual revenue in 20x3; Dec salaries paid Jan 5 \u2192 accrue Dec 31; TV paid Dec 4, delivered Jan 9 \u2192 cash received is a *liability (advance)* until delivery, revenue in 20x4.\n2. **Conservatism (prudence).** Recognize expenses/liabilities as soon as possible under uncertainty; recognize revenues/assets only when assured. Examples: expense the $2M ad campaign immediately; accrue the $5M probable lawsuit loss now; wait until services rendered to record contract revenue. **Exam (QUC 12322):** choose the solution least likely to *overstate assets/income* or *understate liabilities/expenses*.\n3. **Consistency.** Same policies period to period; change only when justified on a permanent basis.\n4. **Matching.** Expenses recognized in the same period as related revenues (COGS when sold; depreciation over useful life; warranty estimate in period of sale; bad debt estimate in year of sale). **Exam (QUC 317):** catalogues printed Dec for a Jan promotion \u2192 deferred cost at Dec 31 because of *matching*. **Exam (QUC 314):** manufacturing labor is capitalized into inventory and expensed when the *product is sold*.\n5. **Accruals:** revenues earned / expenses incurred, cash not yet exchanged.\n6. **Deferrals:** cash paid/received, but revenue/expense belongs to a future period.\n7. **Depreciation:** allocating cost of tangible assets over periods benefited. **Amortization:** allocating costs over periods in which consumed (intangibles).\n\n---\n\n### A.2 Recording Business Transactions\n\n**Double-entry bookkeeping:** every transaction \u22652 entries; total debits = total credits; each account has a left (Dr) and right (Cr) side.\n\n**Account classification and normal balances**\n\n| Account type | Normal balance | Increased by | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Assets | Dr | Dr | Cash, A/R, Inventory, PP&E |\n| Liabilities, Equity | Cr | Cr | A/P, N/P, Common Stock, Retained Earnings |\n| Revenues, Gains | Cr | Cr | Revenue, Gain on Sale of Equipment |\n| Expenses, Losses | Dr | Dr | COGS, Salaries, Depreciation, Loss from Flood |\n| Contra-asset | Cr (shown in assets) | Cr | Accumulated Depreciation, Allowance for Doubtful Debts |\n| Contra-liability | Dr (shown in liabilities) | Dr | Discount on Notes Payable |\n| Adjunct | Same as the account it adds to |  -  | Premium on Notes Payable |\n\n**The accounting cycle (order matters  -  QUC 2638: posting \u2192 adjusting \u2192 closing \u2192 reversing)**\n\n1. **Identify & measure** transactions/events in monetary terms.\n2. **Journalize**  -  journal entry needs: date, \u22651 debit account, \u22651 credit account, equal Dr/Cr amounts, description.\n3. **Post** to the ledger (GL = all accounts; subsidiary ledgers by item; T-account form is easiest for the exam).\n4. **Unadjusted trial balance**  -  NOT a financial statement; a list of all GL balances at a point in time; proves Dr = Cr; only detects errors where debits \u2260 credits.\n5. **Adjusting entries**  -  end of period to align with accrual basis:\n   - Accruals: accrued revenues (Dr Receivable / Cr Revenue), accrued expenses (Dr Expense / Cr Payable).\n   - Deferrals: prepaid expenses (Dr Expense / Cr Prepaid), unearned revenues (Dr Unearned Revenue / Cr Revenue).\n6. **Adjusted trial balance.**\n7. **Financial statements** (IS, BS, CFS, changes in equity).\n8. **Closing nominal (temporary) accounts**  -  revenues and expenses \u2192 Income Summary \u2192 Retained Earnings. After closing, revenue/expense/income summary = 0; Retained Earnings (permanent) carries net income.\n9. **Post-closing trial balance**  -  only permanent (real) accounts: assets, liabilities, equity (incl. contra accounts like Allowance for Doubtful Accounts  -  QUC 12317).\n10. **Reversing entries (optional)**  -  made at the *beginning* of the next period; the *exact opposite* of prior-period adjusting entries (QUC 319).\n\n**Permanent vs. temporary:** Permanent = all assets, liabilities, equity (incl. contra-assets). Temporary = revenues, expenses, gains, losses, owner's drawing/dividends, income summary.\n\n**BestPort worked example (memorize the pattern)**\n\nTransactions \u2192 entries:\n- Owner invests $60,000 cash: Dr Cash / Cr Capital 60,000\n- 3 months' rent prepaid $3,000: Dr Prepaid Rent / Cr Cash\n- Equipment $10,000 cash: Dr Equipment / Cr Cash\n- $9,000 received for future services: Dr Cash / Cr Unearned Revenue\n- Supplies $800 on credit: Dr Supplies / Cr A/P\n- Land $20,000: $7,000 cash + $13,000 note: Dr Land 20,000 / Cr Cash 7,000, Cr N/P 13,000\n- Pay $3,000 of note: Dr N/P / Cr Cash\n- $4,000 cash for services: Dr Cash / Cr Revenue\n- $2,000 invoice for services: Dr A/R / Cr Revenue\n- $1,500 received for next-month services: Dr Cash / Cr Unearned Revenue\n\nAdjusting entries at Dec 31: Rent Expense 1,000 / Prepaid Rent 1,000; Supplies Expense 200 / Supplies 200; Unearned Revenue 3,000 / Revenue 3,000 (one-third of $9,000 earned).\n\nResult: Revenue 9,000 \u2212 Expenses 1,200 = **Net income 7,800**; Total assets = Total L + OE = **86,100**; unadjusted and adjusted TB both total 87,300; post-closing TB totals 86,100.\n\nClosing entries: Dr Revenue 9,000 / Cr Income Summary 9,000; Dr Income Summary 1,200 / Cr Rent Exp 1,000, Cr Supplies Exp 200; Dr Income Summary 7,800 / Cr Retained Earnings 7,800.\n\n**Exam traps**\n- Dr Prepaid Property Taxes / Cr Property Tax Expense \u2192 recognizes a *prepaid (deferred) expense* (QUC 2235).\n- Adjusting entry to accrue revenue on credit sales: Assets \u2191, Liabilities no effect, Equity \u2191 (QUC 3294).\n\n---\n\n### A.3 Types and Elements of Financial Statements\n\n**Elements (definitions to memorize verbatim)**\n- Assets / Liabilities / Equity  -  as in A.1.\n- **Revenues:** inflows or enhancements of assets (or settlement of liabilities) from the entity's *ongoing major/central operations*.\n- **Expenses:** outflows/using up of assets (or incurrence of liabilities) from ongoing major/central operations.\n- **Gains / Losses:** increases/decreases in equity from *peripheral or incidental* transactions (not from revenues/expenses or owner investments/distributions). Key differentiator: the entity's typical activities (laptop maker selling a laptop = revenue; selling its machine above book value = gain).\n\n**Four statements on the FMAA:** Balance Sheet, Income Statement, Statement of Changes in Equity, Statement of Cash Flows. (Out of scope: statement of OCI / comprehensive income / retained earnings.)\n\n**Users:** Internal  -  management (plan/control, assess strength, evaluate performance, allocate resources, financing decisions), employees. External primary  -  investors (risk/return), lenders/creditors (risk rating, ability to pay). Other external  -  government/regulators, unions, financial advisors/rating agencies, stock exchanges, competitors, customers, public.\n\n#### Balance Sheet\n- **Usefulness:** assess operating capability/asset efficiency; capital structure, risk, creditworthiness; enterprise value (with other statements); amounts/timing/uncertainty of future cash flows; **liquidity** (time until asset converts to cash) and **financial flexibility** (ability to alter amounts/timing of cash flows). **Exam (QUC 1752):** the statement of financial position helps evaluate *economic resources and obligations*.\n- **Limitations:** historical cost (not fair value); judgments/estimates (depreciation, useful lives, salvage); omits unrecordable items (human resources); snapshot at a point in time.\n- **Classification:** separate items by type/function, financial-flexibility implications (restricted cash \u2192 other assets), and liquidity (current vs. non-current).\n- **Assets:** Current assets (cash and assets converted/consumed within 1 year or operating cycle, whichever longer; listed by liquidity: cash, marketable securities, receivables, inventories, prepaids) \u2192 Long-term investments \u2192 PP&E (land not depreciable) \u2192 Intangibles (patents, copyrights, franchises, goodwill, trademarks) \u2192 Other assets (deferred charges, non-current receivables, special funds, advances to subsidiaries).\n- **Liabilities:** Current (payables; collections in advance; current portion of LTD; obligations liquidated within the operating cycle). Long-term (bonds, LT notes, leases, pensions, deferred taxes, contingencies such as warranties). Short-term obligations the entity *intends and is able* to refinance long-term (e.g., non-cancelable refinancing agreement) are classified as long-term.\n- **Equity:** capital stock (par/stated value), additional paid-in capital, retained earnings (may be appropriated/restricted by board authorization), accumulated OCI. Disclose **authorized, issued, outstanding** shares. Treasury stock = contra-equity.\n- **Supplemental information:** contingencies; valuation/accounting policies (Summary of Significant Accounting Policies); contractual situations (covenants, leases, pensions, stock options); **post-balance-sheet (subsequent) events**  -  (a) evidence about conditions existing at BS date \u2192 *adjust*; (b) conditions arising after \u2192 *disclose only* (stock issues/splits, business combinations, litigation from post-BS events, fire/flood losses, receivable losses from post-BS conditions).\n- **Disclosure techniques:** parenthetical explanations, notes/footnotes, cross-reference & contra/adjunct items, supporting schedules.\n\n#### Income Statement\n- **Uses:** evaluate past performance, predict future cash flows and their risk, assess profitability/sustainable earnings. **Exam (QUC 1751):** assessing management's *efficient and profitable use of resources* is the IS's job, not the CFS.\n- **Limitations:** affected by accounting methods/estimates (quality of earnings); past-oriented; excludes unmeasurable items (some unrealized gains go to OCI; brand, loyalty).\n- **Formats:** *Single-step* (revenues \u2212 expenses). *Multiple-step* (separates operating from non-operating; matches costs with revenues).\n\nMultiple-step skeleton (Sylven Co.): Sales \u2212 discounts \u2212 returns = **Net sales** \u2212 Cost of sales = **Gross profit** \u2212 Selling expenses \u2212 G&A expenses = **Income from operations** + Other revenues/gains \u2212 Other expenses/losses (interest, loss on sale of land) = **Income from continuing operations before tax** \u2212 Income tax = **Income from continuing operations** \u00b1 **Discontinued operations (net of tax)** = **Net income**.\n\n- **Irregular items:**\n  - *Unusual and/or infrequent gains & losses* (write-downs, restructuring, FX gains/losses, PP&E disposals, strikes, disasters, expropriation) \u2192 reported *before tax* in Other Revenues & Gains / Other Expenses & Losses; **not net of tax**; \"extraordinary items\" presentation no longer permitted under GAAP/IFRS.\n  - *Discontinued operations* \u2192 disposal of a clearly distinguishable segment/product line; shown **net of tax** as a separate category (operations results + gain/loss on disposal). Not discontinued ops: disposing part of a line, relocating production, phasing out a product, technology changes.\n  - Inventory theft by employees (QUC 2507) \u2192 classified as a *loss, separate line item* on the IS.\n- **Changes in accounting estimate** (useful lives, salvage, uncollectibles): prospective (period of change and future); never retroactive; not errors. **Exam (QUC 2793):** a change in collectability of receivables = change in estimate.\n- IS footnotes: depreciation detail, revenue recognition method, tax detail, EPS.\n\n#### Statement of Changes in Equity\nReconciles each equity component from beginning to end: Beginning balance \u00b1 prior-period adjustments + net income + share issuances \u2212 dividends declared = Ending balance.\n\n**Equity-derived net income (QUC 325):** Ending equity = Beginning equity + investments \u2212 withdrawals + NI. (270\u2212171) = (210\u2212120) + 72 \u2212 75 + NI \u2192 99 = 87 + NI \u2192 **NI = 12,000**.\n\n#### Statement of Cash Flows\n- **Purpose:** cash receipts/payments during the period: operating, investing, financing, net change in cash. Helps evaluate **liquidity** (nearness to cash / ability to settle current liabilities), **solvency** (ability to meet liabilities as they mature  -  operating CF is the key factor), **financial flexibility**. Helps assess amount/timing/uncertainty of cash flows, ability to pay obligations, ability to generate future cash flows.\n- **Limitations:** excludes non-cash transactions; classification involves judgment; historical, limited predictive value.\n- **Classifications (memorize):**\n\n| Activity | Inflows | Outflows |\n|---|---|---|\n| Operating | Collections from customers; **interest received**; **dividends received** | Payments to suppliers, employees, taxes; **interest paid** |\n| Investing | Sale of PP&E/long-lived assets; collection of loans made to others; sale of other entities' equity/debt | Purchase of PP&E; loans made to others; purchase of securities |\n| Financing | Issuing stock; borrowing | Repurchasing treasury stock; repaying debt principal; **paying dividends** |\n\nExam checks: equipment purchase $5,000 = investing outflow; bank borrowing $10,000 = financing inflow (QUC 6104). Of dividends paid 150k, interest paid 250k, equipment 350k \u2192 operating = **$250k** (QUC 6106). Purchase of treasury stock = financing (QUC 1960). Credit-card interest paid \u2192 decreases equity, *operating* outflow (QUC 1475).\n\n- **Sources for preparation:** comparative balance sheets (changes), current income statement, selected transaction data.\n- **Operating CF methods:** *Direct* (cash collections/payments  -  rarely used, records not kept). *Indirect* (start with NI, adjust)  -  **only the indirect method is tested on the FMAA**.\n\n**Indirect method  -  adjustments to net income**\n\n| Item | Adjustment |\n|---|---|\n| Non-cash expenses (depreciation, amortization, depletion, impairment, bond discount amortization) | Add |\n| Non-cash revenues (undistributed equity-method earnings) | Subtract |\n| Gains on sale of PP&E / debt extinguishment (investing/financing items) | Subtract |\n| Losses on sale of PP&E / debt extinguishment | Add |\n| Increase in operating current assets (A/R, inventory, prepaids) | Subtract |\n| Decrease in operating current assets | Add |\n| Increase in operating current liabilities (A/P, accruals) | Add |\n| Decrease in operating current liabilities | Subtract |\n\n**Exam (QUC 1881):** income before taxes 60,000 incl. depreciation 5,000, bond discount amortization 6,000, interest expense 4,000 \u2192 estimated cash flow = 60,000 + 5,000 + 6,000 = **71,000** (interest is a cash item  -  no adjustment).\n\n**Worked illustration (memorize the mechanics):** NI 40,000 + depreciation 18,000 \u2212 gain on machine 4,000 + \u2193A/R 5,000 \u2212 \u2191inventory 10,000 \u2212 \u2191prepaids 3,000 \u2212 \u2193A/P 8,000 = **Operating CF 38,000**. Investing: +18,000 sale of machine \u2212 60,000 purchase = **(42,000)**. Financing: +41,000 stock issued \u2212 20,000 loan repaid \u2212 10,000 dividends = **11,000**. Net change = 7,000; 20,000 \u2192 27,000 cash.\n- Depreciation expense derived from accumulated depreciation: \u0394Accum Dep (12,000) + accum dep removed on disposal (20,000 cost \u2212 14,000 BV = 6,000) = 18,000.\n- Dividends derived from retained earnings: \u0394RE 30,000 = NI 40,000 \u2212 dividends \u2192 dividends 10,000.\n\n**Relationships among statements:** NI links IS \u2192 retained earnings (BS); CFS explains change in BS cash; BS equity \u2194 statement of changes in equity; depreciation on IS accumulates in BS contra-asset.\n\n---\n\n### A.4 Internal Controls\n\n**Corporate governance:** the system by which organizations are directed and controlled  -  rules, practices, processes balancing stakeholder interests (board, management, shareholders, creditors, customers, suppliers, regulators, community). Primary goal: enhance long-term shareholder value while considering other stakeholders. Covers monitoring, oversight, assurance, risk management, control, disclosure, ethics, accountability.\n\n**Governance \u2194 risk management \u2194 control:** Governance is the broad framework; risk management (identify, assess, manage, control risks for reasonable assurance of objectives) and control (actions to manage risk and increase likelihood objectives are achieved) are components of it. **Agency problem:** separation of ownership (principals/shareholders) and management (agents) \u2192 board supervision/monitoring needed.\n\n**Internal control definitions**\n- Control = any action by management/board/others to manage risk and provide *reasonable assurance* objectives will be achieved. **Ultimate responsibility: the board; establishing/maintaining the system: senior management.**\n- **COSO definition:** a process, effected by board, management, and personnel, designed to provide reasonable assurance regarding (1) effectiveness & efficiency of operations, (2) reliability of financial reporting, (3) compliance with laws/regulations.\n- **Ultimate objective of managerial control (QUC 6450):** *promote organizational effectiveness*.\n\n**Risk concepts**\n- Risk = probability an event adversely affects the organization.\n- Risk responses: **tolerate/accept, treat (reduce  -  internal controls), transfer, avoid**.\n- Inherent (gross) risk \u2192 managed risk + residual (net) risk. Profits are the reward for residual risks assumed.\n- Financial reporting risk model: **Inherent risk** (susceptibility to misstatement absent controls), **Control risk** (controls fail to prevent/detect/correct timely  -  QUC 128), **Detection risk** (assurance/audit procedures fail to detect). Control risk affected by: control consciousness, design adequacy (segregation of duties), communication, consistent application, frequency of independent checks, independent monitoring.\n- **Reasonable assurance, not absolute:** collusion, management override, and cost/benefit limits mean no system is fault-proof; cost must not exceed benefit (QUC 7645).\n\n**Objectives of IC:** operations (effectiveness/efficiency, safeguarding assets), reporting (reliability, timeliness, transparency), compliance.\n\n**Strong IC benefits:** better decisions, asset protection, lower external audit cost (auditor reliance). **Weak IC exposures:** employee theft, loss of control over information, inefficiency, poor decisions, higher audit fees (substantive approach).\n\n**Organizational structure & IC:** centralization \u2192 standardized, easier-to-enforce controls (decentralized \u2192 diverse, harder to make uniform); clear reporting lines \u2192 easier segregation/checks; size \u2192 larger = more sophisticated. Continuum: rigid structure (army) = least flexible, least risky, most control; flat/free-rein = flexible, risky, less control.\n\n**Management philosophy & IC:** values/operating style shape the control environment; risk-averse \u2192 stringent controls; attitudes toward financial reporting, accounting choices, budgets/standards, auditors, and interaction with personnel (open-door policy, contact with remote offices) all reflect control consciousness. **Exam (QUC 7763):** best indicator of commitment = *funds allocated annually to update/improve information systems*; negatives = decisions controlled by a small top group, aggressive reporting attitudes, strong emphasis on meeting earnings projections.\n\n**Segregation of incompatible duties**  -  separate: (1) **authorization**, (2) **recordkeeping**, (3) **custody** of the asset, (4) **periodic reconciliation**. Does not prevent collusion.\n\nCycle applications:\n- Production: planning vs. custody vs. recording vs. cost accounting vs. reconciliation.\n- Payroll: authorizing rates/deductions, hiring/termination, payroll prep, check distribution, reconciliation to payroll register/HR.\n- Sales-receivables: credit authorization, sale authorization, product custody, cash custody, recordkeeping, A/R-to-cash reconciliation. One person adjusts A/R, a different person posts payments; cash custody separate from write-off authority; deposit preparer \u2260 bank reconciler.\n- Purchases-payables: initiation, receiving, payment authorization, custody, recordkeeping, verification. PO issuer \u2260 receiving recorder (prevents fictitious-vendor fraud).\n- **Exam (QUC 7646):** cashier preparing a weekly deposit slip for all cash receipts = violation (custody + recording combined, and not timely).\n\n**IS environment segregation:** one person/function performs only one of: (1) requirement/change request, (2) authorization/approval, (3) design/development, (4) review/approval, (5) implementation in production.\n\n**Compensating controls** (when duties cannot be separated): audit trails, reconciliation of applications, exception reports handled at supervisory level, transaction logs, supervisory review.\n\n**Safeguarding assets**  -  access limited to authorized personnel (physical and indirect/document access). Controls: segregation; cash registers, lockbox, intact daily deposits, treasury custody of cash; credit-memo and write-off approvals independent of sales/credit/A-R; sequentially pre-numbered forms; three-way match (PO, invoice, receiving report) before payment and voucher cancelation; payroll from supervisor-approved time cards, checks distributed by treasury, unclaimed checks held independently; securities dual-control; production material controls, perpetual records; physical protection from disasters; restricted access + internal audit inspections.\n- Physical access: locks (metal/electronic/biometric), deadman doors, alarms/motion detectors, cameras, guards, color-coded ID badges, escorted visitors, single entry point, bonded personnel.\n- Logical controls: unique passwords, biometrics, securing PC storage, encryption, firewalls, anti-malware, backup/recovery.\n\n**Reconciliation of recorded accountability with assets:** cash/securities counts, bank reconciliations, physical inventories; performed by an *independent* person; frequency depends on nature/amount of asset and cost (count cash daily, not inventory).\n\n---\n\n### A.5 Managing a Company's Daily Finances\n\n**Working capital**\n- Working capital = short-term assets (cash, marketable securities, receivables, inventories, short-term credits). **Net working capital = Current assets \u2212 Current liabilities.**\n- Goal: minimize the **operating cycle** (days to acquire, hold, sell, collect) and the **cash cycle** (operating cycle \u2212 payables days).\n- Liquidity/return trade-off: more liquid assets \u2192 lower returns; shorter-term liabilities \u2192 cheaper. Manage to minimize cost of liquidity while avoiding insolvency.\n- Short-term forecast benefits: visibility of timing/amounts of cash flows; inventory and receivables alignment; supplier negotiation/discount timing; minimize tied-up capital; identify financing needs in advance.\n- **Working-capital policies:** *Aggressive* (finance longer-term assets with current liabilities; higher return, higher risk of insolvency); *Conservative* (high current assets financed long-term; lowest return, lowest risk); *Moderate/maturity-matching* (match asset and liability maturities). **Exam (QUC 6882):** finance a new machine with long-term sources  -  retained earnings or long-term loan (maturity matching).\n- **NWC exam (QUC 12090):** cash\u2193 + inventory\u2191 \u2192 no change; cash\u2193 + A/P\u2193 \u2192 no change; **cash\u2193 + fixed assets\u2191 \u2192 NWC decreases**; cash\u2191 + LTD\u2191 \u2192 NWC increases.\n\n**Cash management**\n- Cash is most liquid, lowest return; hold enough, not too much.\n- **Motives for holding cash: transaction, speculation, precautionary** (NOT \"to make a profit\"  -  QUC 6189).\n- Factors affecting cash level: revenue patterns (seasonality), operating expenses, contingencies, growth opportunities, market conditions.\n- Cash forecasts identify deficits (arrange short-term financing) and surpluses (invest).\n\n**Accounts receivable management**\n- A/R = oral promises; current; recorded net of trade discounts.\n- Credit policy (set by top management, implemented by credit department): credit period, credit standards, credit limits, discount % and period. **Default (credit/bankruptcy) risk** must be balanced against added sales.\n- Effects of loosening: longer credit period \u2192 \u2191sales, \u2191A/R, \u2191default risk & carrying cost; higher cash discount \u2192 faster collection, maybe \u2191sales, cost of discount; relaxed standards \u2192 \u2191sales, \u2191A/R, \u2191bad debts & collection costs.\n- **Factoring** = selling A/R below face value to a factor (QUC 589). *With recourse*  -  seller guarantees; treated as a sale only if seller surrenders control, recourse obligation reasonably estimable, and factor cannot force repurchase; otherwise a borrowing. *Without recourse*  -  factor bears default risk (higher cost); Dr Cash, Dr Loss on Sale of Receivables, Cr A/R.\n\n**Inventory management**\n- Types: raw materials, work-in-process, finished goods, materials & supplies (not traceable).\n- Reasons to hold: supply/demand differences, process delays, quantity discounts, level operations, avoid stockouts.\n- Two questions: when to order, how much.\n- **Costs:** *ordering* (placing orders, lost discounts), *holding/carrying* (storage, freight-in & insurance on shipment, facility depreciation/rent, insurance, property taxes, security, spoilage/theft/obsolescence, opportunity cost)  -  **not shipping-out costs (QUC 6214)**; *stockout* (lost goodwill/profits)  -  consider **lead time** (order placed \u2192 received & ready).\n- Passing tip: business cycles, seasonal demand, econometric modeling help forecast inventory needs.\n- **JIT:** demand-driven, inventory arrives when needed, no warehousing; cuts carrying costs, theft risk, control needs, property taxes; needs reliable suppliers (small safety stock in practice); exposes material-usage inefficiencies and supplier defects; often integrated with MRP. **Passing tip: JIT \u2192 \u2191stockout costs, \u2193carrying costs.**\n- **MRP:** computer-based; converts master production schedule into purchasing/production schedule; tracks levels, on-order quantities, lead times, usage; benefits  -  optimal levels, better scheduling, lower costs (fewer rush orders/overtime).\n\n**Accounts payable management**\n- A/P = trade credit borrowing; use only when cost of trade credit < other funding; cost depends on cash discount offered.\n- **A/P cycle:** purchase requisition \u2192 PO (legally binding) \u2192 validate goods received (vs. PO & packing slip; receiving report) \u2192 vendor invoice \u2192 invoice validation/three-way matching \u2192 approval \u2192 record liability \u2192 payment authorization \u2192 payment processing \u2192 documentation & reconciliation (incl. vendor statements) \u2192 payment confirmation.\n- Benefits: access to trade credit, avoid late charges, invoice tracking, fraud prevention (duplicate invoices, unauthorized payments), smoother cash flow, supplier relationships, better reporting.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION B  -  FINANCIAL STATEMENT ANALYSIS\n\n### B.1 Recognition and Valuation in Financial Statements\n\n#### Accounts receivable\n- Trade (A/R = oral promise, current; N/R = written promise, current or noncurrent by maturity) vs. nontrade (employee advances, dividends/interest receivable  -  report separately).\n- **Cash (sales) discounts, e.g., 2/10, n/30.**\n  - *Gross method:* record at gross; discount recognized when taken (Sales Discount is a deduction from sales). $5,000 sale; $2,000 paid in period: Dr Cash 1,960, Dr Sales Discount 40, Cr A/R 2,000.\n  - *Net method:* record at net (4,900); discount forfeited on late payment \u2192 Cr **Sales Discount Forfeited** (Other Revenues): Dr A/R 60 / Cr Sales Discount Forfeited 60, then Dr Cash 3,000 / Cr A/R 3,000.\n- **Valuation:** short-term receivables at **net realizable value**; noncurrent at **present (discounted) value**. Disclose expected losses, nontrade amounts, pledged/discounted amounts.\n- **Allowance method** for uncollectibles (Dr Bad Debt Expense / Cr Allowance for Doubtful Debts):\n  - *Percentage of sales (income-statement approach):* expense = % \u00d7 credit sales (1% \u00d7 $10M = $100,000) regardless of existing allowance.\n  - *Percentage of receivables (balance-sheet approach)/aging:* compute **required ending allowance** (aging: 420,000\u00d73% + 18,000\u00d710% + 14,000\u00d730% + 50,000\u00d775% = 56,100); expense = required \u2212 existing balance (56,100 \u2212 12,500 = **43,600**).\n  - Write-off: Dr Allowance / Cr A/R (no income effect). Recovery: reverse write-off (Dr A/R / Cr Allowance) then Dr Cash / Cr A/R.\n  - A/R roll-forward: Beg + credit sales \u2212 collections \u2212 write-offs = End. Allowance roll-forward: Beg + bad debt expense + recoveries \u2212 write-offs = End.\n- **Exam (QUC 7398):** collection pattern 60/25/15 \u2192 May 1 A/R = 15% of March sales + 40% of April sales = 0.15\u00d7280,000 + 0.40\u00d7260,000 = **146,000** (discount ignored for the balance).\n\n#### Inventory\n- Types as in A.5 (finished goods may be on consignment).\n- **Inventory (product) costs  -  capitalize:** purchase price, freight-in, insurance in transit, production costs (DM, DL, factory OH), sometimes procurement/purchasing dept costs, interest on self-constructed/discrete-project assets.\n- **Period costs  -  expense:** selling, G&A, **freight-out**, general interest.\n- **Periodic system:** Purchases account; COGS = Beginning inventory + Purchases \u2212 Purchase returns & allowances \u2212 Ending inventory (physical count). **Perpetual:** records updated with each purchase/sale; annual count compared to records.\n- Items to include in count: **consigned goods \u2192 consignor's inventory** (at cost incl. freight to consignee); goods in transit **FOB shipping point \u2192 buyer's**; **FOB destination \u2192 seller's**.\n- **Cost flow assumptions** (FMAA requires FIFO and LIFO only):\n  - **FIFO:** oldest costs to COGS, newest in ending inventory; periodic = perpetual results; **rising prices \u2192 lowest COGS, highest NI, highest ending inventory**.\n  - **LIFO:** newest costs to COGS, oldest remain; periodic \u2260 perpetual possible; **rising prices \u2192 highest COGS, lowest NI**.\n  - Parts Co. example: 2,000 @5.00, +1,500 @5.40, sell 700, +400 @5.65, sell 1,300 \u2192 FIFO COGS 10,000 / EI 10,360; LIFO (periodic and perpetual here) COGS 10,860 / EI 9,500.\n  - **Exam (QUC 12113):** FIFO ending inventory of 11,500 units when 70,100 purchased at $7.50 \u2192 all from purchases = 11,500 \u00d7 7.50 = **86,250**.\n\n#### PP&E and depreciation\n- Record at cost = all necessary costs until ready for intended use: purchase price, property taxes/import duties, transportation-in, installation and trial runs, construction-period interest (expense interest after ready).\n- Depreciation = systematic, rational allocation of tangible-asset cost to periods benefited. Base data: cost 1,000,000; salvage 100,000; life 5 yrs; 20,000 hours.\n\n| Method | Formula | Year-1 result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Activity (units/hours) | (Cost \u2212 Salvage) \u00f7 total capacity \u00d7 usage | 900,000/20,000 = $45/hr \u00d7 3,000 = **135,000** |\n| Straight-line | (Cost \u2212 Salvage) \u00f7 life | 900,000/5 = **180,000** |\n| Sum-of-years'-digits | (Cost \u2212 Salvage) \u00d7 remaining life \u00f7 SYD (5+4+3+2+1=15) | 900,000\u00d75/15 = **300,000**; then 240k, 180k, 120k, 60k |\n| Double-declining balance | Book value (NOT less salvage) \u00d7 2 \u00d7 SL rate; stop at salvage | 1,000,000\u00d740% = **400,000**; then 240k, 144k, 86.4k, final 29.6k (to reach BV 100,000) |\n\n**Passing tip  -  SL vs. DB:** early years DB has higher depreciation, lower NI; later years reversed; accumulated depreciation higher under DB, retained earnings lower; gross fixed assets same.\n\n**Exam (QUC 7000):** SYD, cost 250,000, salvage 50,000, 5 yrs \u2192 Year 3 expense 200,000\u00d73/15 = **40,000**; accumulated after 3 yrs = 200,000\u00d712/15 = **160,000**.\n\n#### Intangible assets\n- Nonmonetary, no physical substance (patents, copyrights, franchises, goodwill, trademarks, licenses, domain names). Long-term.\n- Purchased \u2192 at cost (incl. costs to prepare for use); acquired by exchange \u2192 fair value of consideration given or asset received (more reliable).\n- Internally created \u2192 cost = only direct costs (legal fees); **R&D expensed as incurred, never capitalized**; **internally generated goodwill never capitalized** (only via acquisition).\n- Finite life \u2192 amortize (cost \u2212 residual) over useful life; carrying value = cost \u2212 accumulated amortization \u2212 impairment. Indefinite life \u2192 not amortized; impairment test at least annually.\n\n#### Asset retirement\n- By sale, exchange, abandonment, involuntary conversion. Depreciate up to retirement date; remove asset and accumulated depreciation; proceeds vs. book value \u2192 gain/loss, shown **below operating income within continuing operations**.\n- Entry (gain): Dr Cash/New asset, Dr Accumulated Depreciation, Cr Asset, Cr Gain. (Loss): also Dr Loss.\n- **Exam (QUC 1478):** cost 700,000 \u2212 accum dep 495,000 = BV 205,000; gain 75,000 \u2192 selling price = **280,000**.\n\n#### Liabilities\n- Current vs. long-term definitions as in A.3. A liability due within the year is **excluded from current liabilities** if (1) it will be settled from non-current assets (dedicated fund), or (2) the company has **both intention and ability to refinance** (actual long-term refinancing before issuance, or non-cancelable refinancing agreement). **Exam (QUC 546):** both the $140,000 (refinanced Feb 1) and $200,000 (non-cancelable agreement Feb 7) notes excluded \u2192 **$340,000**.\n- **Short-term financing sources:** trade credit; unsecured bank loans (promissory note, line of credit, revolving credit agreement = guaranteed line; compensating balances raise effective rate); commercial paper; secured loans (pledged A/R or inventory  -  field warehousing, warehouse receipts; chattel mortgages on movable property; repurchase agreements); factoring; bankers' acceptances.\n- **Long-term:** term loans, leases, bonds (large reputable corporations); higher rates for maturity uncertainty.\n\n#### Revenue recognition (ASC 606 / IFRS 15  -  \"Revenue from Contracts with Customers\")\n- **Asset-liability approach** replaces \"earned and realized.\" **Core principle:** recognize revenue to depict transfer of promised goods/services in the amount of consideration the entity expects to be entitled to.\n- **Five steps:** (1) identify the contract; (2) identify performance obligations; (3) determine the transaction price; (4) allocate the price to obligations; (5) recognize revenue when/as obligations are satisfied.\n- **Step 1  -  valid contract criteria:** approved & committed; rights identifiable; payment terms identifiable; commercial substance; collection probable. If not met and cash received \u2192 revenue only when consideration is nonrefundable and no remaining obligations or contract terminated; otherwise a liability. Cash received before performance \u2192 **Unearned revenue** (Company A: $9,000 received May 1 \u2192 liability; June 15 delivery \u2192 revenue 9,000, COGS 6,000).\n- **Step 2  -  performance obligation** = promise to transfer a *distinct* good/service (or a series of substantially similar ones). Not distinct if interdependent/integrated (software + extensive customization = one obligation; software + separately sold support = two).\n- **Step 3  -  transaction price** (excludes amounts collected for third parties, e.g., sales tax). Consider:\n  - *Variable consideration:* **expected value** (probability-weighted; many outcomes) or **most likely amount** (two outcomes). Example: 50%\u00d7360k + 30%\u00d7320k + 20%\u00d7300k = **336,000**.\n  - *Significant financing component* (>1 year): revenue at cash-selling price; difference = interest. $13,310 due in 3 yrs, cash price $10,000 \u2192 Dr A/R 13,310, Cr Unearned Interest Revenue 3,310, Cr Sales 10,000; interest 1,000 / 1,100 / 1,210 at 10%.\n  - *Noncash consideration:* fair value at inception.\n  - *Consideration payable to customer* (discounts, rebates, coupons): reduces transaction price unless for a distinct good/service. 5% probable volume rebate on $200,000 \u2192 revenue **190,000**.\n- **Step 4  -  allocate on relative standalone selling prices.** $300,000 contract; SSP license 250,000 + support 3\u00d750,000 = 400,000 \u2192 license 187,500; support 112,500.\n- **Step 5  -  recognize when control transfers** (ability to direct use and obtain benefits).\n  - *Over time* if any: customer simultaneously receives/consumes benefits (routine services); entity's performance creates/enhances a customer-controlled asset (building on customer's land); no alternative use **and** enforceable right to payment for performance to date. Measure progress by **output** (units delivered, milestones, appraisals) or **input** (costs incurred, labor/machine hours; **cost-to-cost** most common; straight-line if inputs even). Support revenue 112,500/3 = 37,500 per year.\n  - *Point in time* otherwise  -  control indicators: right to payment, legal title, physical possession, risks and rewards, customer acceptance. Warehouse with only a refundable deposit and no right to payment for work to date \u2192 point in time; with progress payments, no alternative use, and full right to consideration \u2192 over time.\n- **Exam (QUC 11810):** 100,000 gallons at $3; 50,000 delivered by year-end \u2192 revenue **150,000** regardless of cash received. **(QUC 525):** annual lawn-service fee paid in advance \u2192 recognize *over the year as services are performed*.\n- **Specific applications:**\n  - *Right of return:* revenue only for units expected to be kept; **refund liability** (allowance for sales returns, contra-revenue); asset for expected recovered inventory with COGS adjustment. 40 units @1,000 (cost 800), 4 expected returns \u2192 net sales 36,000.\n  - *Repurchase agreements:* forward/call \u2192 **lease** if repurchase price < original price; **financing** if \u2265 original price. Put option \u2192 sale with right of return if no significant incentive to exercise; lease if incentive and repurchase price < original; financing if repurchase price \u2265 original and > expected market value.\n  - *Bill-and-hold:* revenue when control passes and all of: substantive reason, product separately identified, ready for transfer, entity cannot use/redirect it.\n  - *Principal vs. agent:* principal controls the good/service before transfer \u2192 **gross** revenue; agent arranges \u2192 **net** commission. Agent indicators: another party primarily responsible, no inventory risk, no pricing discretion, commission-based consideration, no credit risk.\n  - *Consignments:* consignor keeps title and inventory (Inventory-consignment incl. freight); revenue only on notification of sale; consignee records commission revenue and a payable to consignor. COGS = proportion of cost sold \u00d7 (cost + freight): (10,000/30,000)\u00d731,500 = 10,500.\n\n#### Expense recognition (three methods)\n1. **Associating cause and effect (matching):** COGS, sales-return allowance, bad-debt allowance, warranty accrual  -  same period as related revenue.\n2. **Systematic and rational allocation:** depreciation, prepaid insurance, interest and operating lease costs by passage of time.\n3. **Immediate recognition:** officers' salaries, G&A, assets that no longer provide benefit (worthless patent).\n\n#### Equity transactions\n- Components: contributed capital (capital stock at par + additional paid-in capital), retained earnings (free and appropriated), accumulated OCI, treasury stock (contra-equity).\n- **Common stock:** voting rights, residual claim; par, no-par (avoids state taxes/arbitrary par), book value = (assets \u2212 liabilities \u2212 preferred) \u00f7 common shares, market value. Issue 1,000 shares $50 par at $75: Dr Cash 75,000 / Cr Common Stock 50,000 / Cr APIC 25,000.\n- **Pre-emptive rights:** buy new issues pro rata. Value of a right (rights-on) = (share price rights-on \u2212 subscription price) \u00f7 (rights needed per share + 1). Shares trade rights-on before issuance, ex-rights after.\n- **Preferred stock:** dividend preference and priority in liquidation (after debt, before common); usually no vote (may vote on mergers); dividends not guaranteed until declared; may be convertible, callable/redeemable, with sinking fund, exchangeable for bonds, participating; par used for dividend % and liquidation. **Cumulative** \u2192 dividends in arrears (not a liability until declared) must be paid before common; **noncumulative** \u2192 only current year.\n- **Treasury stock:** repurchased shares; issued but *not outstanding* (no dividends, no votes); reasons  -  improve EPS/ROE, support share price, supply shares for compensation/stock dividends. Dr Treasury Stock / Cr Cash; reduces assets and equity; not an asset.\n- **Prior-period adjustments** (error corrections, e.g., cash \u2192 accrual, unrecorded expense): adjust **opening retained earnings, net of tax**; excluded from current NI.\n- **Changes in accounting principle** (average cost \u2192 FIFO; completed-contract \u2192 percentage-of-completion): cumulative effect, net of tax, to beginning retained earnings (retroactive computation). **Change in depreciation method = change in estimate** (prospective).\n- **Stock split:** memorandum entry only; 4:1 split at $100 \u2192 ~$25. Reverse split consolidates shares.\n- **Cash dividends:** liability only when **declared**  -  Dr Retained Earnings / Cr Dividends Payable; on payment Dr Dividends Payable / Cr Cash.\n- **Stock dividends:** *small (<20-25%)* \u2192 RE reduced at **fair market value** (Dr RE at FMV, Cr Common Stock at par, Cr APIC for excess). *Large (>20-25%)* \u2192 RE reduced at **par**. **Exam (QUC 12115):** 5% \u00d7 100,000 shares \u00d7 $18 = RE decrease **90,000**. **Exam (QUC 11813):** cash dividend distributed (already removed from equity at declaration) + stock dividend declared \u2192 total equity change **$0**.\n- **Accumulated OCI:** gains/losses not in NI; bypass IS and RE; permanent balance-sheet equity account. Sample equity section: common stock 50,000 + APIC 100,000 + RE 20,000 \u2212 treasury (16,000) + AOCI 3,000 = 157,000.\n\n---\n\n### B.2 Basic Financial Statement Analysis\n\n- **Financial statement analysis:** evaluate risk and performance; identify strengths/weaknesses. Tools: ratio analysis (relationships between statement items; compare with other firms, industry, own history) and trend analysis (successive periods; direction and amount of change).\n- **Common-size statements:** each element as a % of a base \u2192 comparable across companies of different sizes.\n  - **Vertical:** each item \u00f7 base *for the same year*. Base = **total assets** (balance sheet  -  QUC 2419) and **net sales** (income statement). Used to compare periods, companies, and industry averages. \"Advertising is 2% of sales\" = vertical (QUC 6910). Net income 120,000 \u00f7 sales 600,000 = **20%** (QUC 12057).\n  - **Horizontal (common base year / trend / variation analysis):** each item as a % of the *base-year* amount \u2192 growth rates of individual line items (125% \u2192 25% growth). Inventory 207.46 \u2192 107.46% growth. Largest % change example (QUC 12059): inventory 500/600 = 83% \u2192 17% change beats sales 110%, NI 108%, assets 105%. Growth rate = (current \u00f7 prior \u00d7 100) \u2212 100: 622,310/568,920 \u2192 **9.38%** (QUC 12182).\n- Future Tech illustration takeaways: rising inventories and receivables as % of assets plus collapsing cash %, ballooning A/P and overdrafts, and gross margin sliding 25% \u2192 22% \u2192 19% while G&A jumps to 7% of sales \u2192 operating CF far below net income. Read vertical and horizontal statements together to spot deteriorating quality of earnings.\n\n---\n\n### B.3 Financial Statement Ratio Analysis\n\nRatios compare numbers to test reasonableness, compare across periods/firms/industry. Ratios can be financial or non-financial (labor-hour usage, defect rates). Four FMAA groups: **liquidity, leverage, activity, profitability.** Use **360 days** and *average* balances where the formula says so.\n\n#### Liquidity ratios (short-term obligations)\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Current ratio | Current assets \u00f7 Current liabilities | Most common short-term solvency measure; too high = idle current assets |\n| Quick (acid-test) ratio | (Current assets \u2212 Inventories \u2212 Prepayments) \u00f7 CL = (Cash + Marketable securities + Receivables) \u00f7 CL | Better indicator than current ratio; excludes inventory (libraries, department stores) |\n| Cash ratio | (Cash + Marketable securities) \u00f7 CL | No reliance on inventory or receivables |\n| Cash flow ratio | Cash flow from operations \u00f7 Average CL | Ability to cover current debt from operations |\n| Net working capital | Current assets \u2212 Current liabilities | Absolute liquidity |\n| NWC ratio | NWC \u00f7 Total assets | |\n\n**Exam (QUC 11968):** CL 2,000,000; NWC 300,000 \u2192 CA 2,300,000; quick 0.8 = (2,300,000 \u2212 inventory)/2,000,000 \u2192 inventory **700,000**.\n\n#### Leverage ratios (capital structure, solvency)\n- **Capital structure** = debt + equity mix; more debt \u2192 higher returns/opportunity, higher default risk (fixed charges). **Solvency** = ability to meet long-term liabilities. **Financial leverage** = degree of debt use; interest is tax-deductible (dividends are not), magnifies gains and losses. **Operating leverage** = effect of sales changes on operating income from fixed-cost structure (high fixed costs \u2192 larger swings).\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total debt to total assets | Total debt \u00f7 Total assets | Total debt = CL + LTD; creditors prefer low; owners prefer higher; cyclical industries keep lower ratios |\n| Debt/equity | Total debt \u00f7 Common stockholders' equity | Optimal D/E minimizes WACC |\n| Long-term debt to equity | (Total debt \u2212 CL) \u00f7 Common stockholders' equity | |\n| Times interest earned (interest coverage) | EBIT \u00f7 Interest expense | High TIE with high debt ratio preferred by lenders over low TIE with low debt ratio; 20M \u00f7 5M = **4** (QUC 11969) |\n\n#### Activity ratios (asset management)\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Inventory turnover | COGS \u00f7 Average inventory | Higher = better capital use |\n| Days sales in inventory | Average inventory \u00f7 Average daily COGS = 360 \u00f7 Inventory turnover | Purchase \u2192 sale time; watch stockout costs |\n| A/R turnover | Net credit sales \u00f7 Average trade receivables | 3,600,000 \u00f7 [(320,000+400,000)/2] = **10** (QUC 6026) |\n| Days sales outstanding (DSO) | Average trade receivables \u00f7 Average daily sales = 360 \u00f7 A/R turnover | High = deteriorating liquidity; too low = credit too tight |\n| A/P turnover | Net credit purchases \u00f7 Average A/P | Increase = paying faster |\n| Days purchases in A/P | Average A/P \u00f7 Average daily purchases = 360 \u00f7 A/P turnover | |\n| **Operating cycle** | Days sales in inventory + Days sales in receivables | Shorter is favorable |\n| **Cash (conversion) cycle** | Days in inventory + Days in receivables \u2212 Days purchases in payables | Cash-to-cash |\n| Fixed assets turnover | Sales \u00f7 Average fixed assets | Plant efficiency |\n| Total assets turnover | Sales \u00f7 Average total assets | **Exam (QUC 140):** unexpected *decrease* may signal fictitious inventory (inventory inflates assets; current ratio would rise, DSO and P/E unaffected) |\n\n#### Profitability ratios (combined effect of liquidity, asset and debt management)\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Gross profit margin % | Gross profit \u00f7 Net sales | Efficiency of COGS management |\n| Operating profit margin % | Operating income \u00f7 Net sales | Falling relative to GPM \u2192 poor control of other operating costs |\n| Net profit margin | Net income \u00f7 Net sales | Income per $ of sales |\n| Return on assets (ROA) | Net income \u00f7 Average total assets | Earning power after all expenses, taxes, interest |\n| Return on common equity (ROE) | (Net income \u2212 Preferred dividends) \u00f7 Average common equity | **ROE > ROA \u2192 leverage used effectively.** Beechwood: 96,000 \u00f7 avg equity [(300,000+12,000+155,000)+(300,000+28,000+206,000)]/2 = 500,500 \u2192 **19.2%** (QUC 6068; include reserve for bond retirement in equity, exclude bonds) |\n\n---\n\n## RAPID-REVIEW CHEAT SHEET\n\n**Accounting cycle order:** identify \u2192 journalize \u2192 post \u2192 unadjusted TB \u2192 adjust \u2192 adjusted TB \u2192 statements \u2192 close \u2192 post-closing TB \u2192 (reverse).\n\n**Indirect-method sign rules:** + non-cash expenses, + losses, \u2212 gains, \u2212 \u2191operating assets, + \u2193operating assets, + \u2191operating liabilities, \u2212 \u2193operating liabilities. Interest paid/received and dividends received = operating; dividends paid = financing.\n\n**Segregate:** authorization / recordkeeping / custody / reconciliation. Board = ultimate IC responsibility; management establishes and maintains.\n\n**Risk vocabulary:** inherent \u2192 control \u2192 detection; responses: accept, treat, transfer, avoid; residual (net) risk = what remains and earns profit.\n\n**Working-capital styles:** aggressive (ST financing of LT assets), conservative (LT financing of CA), maturity matching.\n\n**Cash motives:** transaction, speculation, precautionary.\n\n**Inventory cost families:** ordering, carrying (storage, insurance, taxes, obsolescence, opportunity), stockout. JIT: \u2193carrying, \u2191stockout.\n\n**A/R allowance:** % of sales \u2192 expense directly; % of receivables/aging \u2192 required ending balance \u2212 existing balance = expense.\n\n**Inventory in rising prices:** FIFO \u2192 high EI, low COGS, high NI; LIFO \u2192 the reverse. FOB shipping point \u2192 buyer's inventory in transit; FOB destination \u2192 seller's. Consigned goods \u2192 consignor.\n\n**Depreciation:** SL = (C\u2212S)/n; SYD fraction = remaining life / n(n+1)/2; DDB = BV \u00d7 2/n (ignore salvage until floor); activity = (C\u2212S)/capacity \u00d7 usage.\n\n**Revenue 5 steps:** contract \u2192 obligations \u2192 price (expected value / most likely; financing >1yr; rebates reduce) \u2192 allocate on SSP \u2192 recognize on control transfer (over time vs. point in time). Agent = net commission; consignor recognizes on notification; bill-and-hold needs four extra criteria.\n\n**Dividends:** cash \u2192 liability at declaration; small stock dividend at FMV; large at par; splits = memo only; stock dividends and splits never change total equity.\n\n**Equity changes:** error correction \u2192 prior-period adjustment to opening RE (net of tax); change in principle \u2192 cumulative effect to opening RE; change in estimate (incl. depreciation method) \u2192 prospective.\n\n**Ratio formulas:** Current = CA/CL; Quick = (CA \u2212 Inv \u2212 Prepaids)/CL; Cash ratio = (Cash + MS)/CL; Debt/assets; Debt/equity; TIE = EBIT/Interest; Inventory turnover = COGS/Avg Inv; A/R turnover = Credit sales/Avg A/R; A/P turnover = Credit purchases/Avg A/P; days = 360/turnover; Operating cycle = DSI + DSO; Cash cycle = DSI + DSO \u2212 DPO; Asset turnover = Sales/Avg assets; GPM, OPM, NPM = margin/Net sales; ROA = NI/Avg assets; ROE = (NI \u2212 Pref div)/Avg common equity.\n\n**Common-size bases:** balance sheet \u2192 total assets; income statement \u2192 net sales. Vertical = same-year %, horizontal = base-year % (growth = index \u2212 100).\n";
